# Tell the client why a payload over the server's limit was refused.
# The server reads a payload's size before the payload and refused one
# over max-file-payload-size (or max-memory-payload-size) by logging
# the reason and closing: it sent no reply. The bridge went on copying
# the upload until the server's connection closed under it, and the
# client's forwarding child discarded the rest, so the client read its
# whole file to the end before meeting the end of its inner TLS session
# and reporting "I/O error: Unexpected EOF in NSPR". The server now
# sends an error reply naming the payload's size and the limit, and
# discards what still arrives until the bridge closes. The bridge
# relays a reply that arrives while it is copying the request payload,
# and stops copying. It waits on both peers meanwhile, and takes from
# the client what has arrived without waiting for more, so that a
# client which has stopped sending, even part-way through a TLS record,
# does not hold the reply up; it counts the idle deadline and the
# throughput floor itself while it does. It gives the client a bounded
# time to close, dropping what it sends meanwhile as it arrives. The
# client stops sending when a reply arrives and reports it, and looks
# for one when its inner session ends before it began.
# The reply cannot be authenticated - its keys travel in the inner
# session a refused request never opens - so the client uses it for its
# error message alone, and prints of that message nothing but printable
# ASCII. See patches/README.md.
#
